Raw denial, captured 2026-09-10. Cookies, CDN and cache headers stripped; nothing else.

$ curl -i -X POST https://api.privy.io/v1/wallets/diefzzkn3f5ltrcoqh1319ij/rpc \
    -u <app id>:<app secret> -H 'privy-app-id: <app id>' \
    -d '{"method":"eth_signTransaction","chain_type":"ethereum",
         "params":{"transaction":{"to":"0x000000000000000000000000000000000000dEaD",
                                  "value":"0x0","chain_id":5042002,...}}}'

HTTP/2 400 
date: Thu, 10 Sep 2026 18:21:40 GMT
content-type: application/json; charset=utf-8
content-length: 80
cache-control: public, max-age=0, must-revalidate
referrer-policy: strict-origin-when-cross-origin
strict-transport-security: max-age=63072000
x-content-type-options: nosniff
x-frame-options: DENY

{"error":"RPC request denied due to policy violation","code":"policy_violation"}

The response does not name the rule that fired. That is deliberate on Privy's side: a
caller must not be able to enumerate a denylist by probing it. Attribution comes from the
control run in 03-denial.json instead — same wallet, same policy, same execution.
